The crossroads lies quiet. Dust lifts in a slow spiral where you pivoted, but no blade, claw, or spell follows. Just the wind and the smell of hot pine sap.

A low growl rips the hush. From the scrub oak south of the track a Hollow Stalker stumbles forward—ribs showing through matted fur, eyes filmed white. It is sick, starving, and still deadly. Captain Oren jogs ten paces behind, spear leveled. “Focus. Don’t panic. Strike when it moves—not before.”

The beast lunges. You sidestep, feel its ragged claws graze your sleeve, then drive your boot into its knee. Bone cracks. It sprawls, hissing, and you bring your weapon down hard between shoulder and spine. The body twitches twice and is still.

Oren exhales, wipes his brow with the back of his glove. “First blood. Nasty thing, but it’s done.” The clearing smells of iron and pine. Your pulse hammers in your ears, proof you’re still breathing.

